"Internal Error ()\nciteturn13view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1162/REST.2003.85.1.226\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://doi.org/10.1162/REST.2003.85.1.226 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn13view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://direct.mit.edu/rest/article/85/1/226/57380/The-Small-Sample-Bias-of-the-Gini-Coefficient-Results\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://direct.mit.edu/rest/article/85/1/226/57380/The-Small-Sample-Bias-of-the-Gini-Coefficient-Results is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn13view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0708155105\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0708155105: (500) Redirected to https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.0708155105 which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[0706.1062] Power-law distributions in empirical data (https://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1062)\nciteturn13view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1062\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 174\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Physics > Data Analysis, Statistics and Probability\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 7 Jun 2007 (【5†v1】), last revised 2 Feb 2009 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Power-law distributions in empirical data\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Aaron Clauset】, 【7†Cosma Rohilla Shalizi】, 【8†M. E. J. Newman】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Power-law distributions in empirical data, by Aaron Clauset and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【9†View PDF】 【10†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract: Power-law distributions occur in many situations of scientific interest and have significant consequences for our understanding of natural and man-made phenomena. Unfortunately, the detection and characterization of power laws is complicated by the large fluctuations that occur in the tail of the distribution -- the part of the distribution representing large but rare events -- and by the difficulty of identifying the range over which power-law behavior holds.\nL17: Commonly used methods for analyzing power-law data, such as least-squares fitting, can produce substantially inaccurate estimates of parameters for power-law distributions, and even in cases where such methods return accurate answers they are still unsatisfactory because they give no indication of whether the data obey a power law at all. Here we present a principled statistical framework for discerning and quantifying power-law behavior in empirical data.\nL18: Our approach combines maximum-likelihood fitting methods with goodness-of-fit tests based on the Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic and likelihood ratios. We evaluate the effectiveness of the approach with tests on synthetic data and give critical comparisons to previous approaches. We also apply the proposed methods to twenty-four real-world data sets from a range of different disciplines, each of which has been conjectured to follow a power-law distribution.\nL19: In some cases we find these conjectures to be consistent with the data while in others the power law is ruled out.\nL20: Comments:  | 43 pages, 11 figures, 7 tables, 4 appendices; code available at 【11†this http URL†www.santafe.edu】\nL21: Subjects:  | Data Analysis, Statistics and Probability (physics.data-an); Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn); Applications (stat.AP); Methodology (stat.ME)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【12†arXiv:0706.1062】 [physics.data-an]\nL