work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL164: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? 【50†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org】.\nL165: \nL166: 【51†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【52†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL167: \nL168: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【53†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL169: 【54†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【55†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【56†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【57†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【58†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【59†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【60†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【61†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL170: \nL171: Major funding support from\nL172: \nL173: 【62†(opens in new tab)†www.simonsfoundation.org】 【63†(opens in new tab)†www.sfi.org.bm】 【64†(opens in new tab)†www.schmidtsciences.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://ccl.northwestern.edu/papers/wolfsheep.pdf)\nciteturn13view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 53; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ccl.northwestern.edu/papers/wolfsheep.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1262\nL0@P0: Thinking Like a Wolf 1\nL1@P0: RUNNING HEAD: Thinking Like a Wolf, a Sheep, or a Firefly\nL2@P0: Thinking Like a Wolf, a Sheep, or a Firefly:\nL3@P0: Learning Biology Through Constructing and Testing\nL4@P0: Computational Theories--an Embodied Modeling Approach\nL5@P0: Uri Wilensky – uri@northwestern.edu\nL6@P0: Northwestern University\nL7@P0: Kenneth Reisman\nL8@P0: Stanford University\nL9@P0-1: To be published in Cognition and Instruction, 2006Thinking Like a Wolf 2\nL10@P1: Abstract\nL11@P1: Biological phenomena can be investigated at multiple levels, from the molecular to the cellular to the\nL12@P1: organismic to the ecological. In typical biology instruction, these levels have been segregated. Yet, it\nL13@P1: is by examining the connections between such levels that many phenomena in biology, and complex\nL14@P1: systems in general, are best explained. We describe a computation-based approach that enables\nL15@P1: students to investigate the connections between different biological levels. Using agent-based,\nL16@P1: embodied modeling tools, students model the micro-rules underlying a biological phenomenon, and\nL17@P1: observe the resultant aggregate dynamics. We describe two cases in which this approach was\nL18@P1: employed. In both cases, students frame hypotheses, construct multi-agent models that incorporate\nL19@P1: these hypotheses, and test these by running their models and observing the outcomes. Contrasting\nL20@P1: these cases against traditionally employed, classical equations-based approaches, we argue that the\nL21@P1: embodied modeling approach connects more directly to students’ experience, enables extended\nL22@P1: investigations as well as deeper understanding, and enables “advanced” topics to be productively\nL23@P1-2: introduced into the high school curriculum.Thinking Like a Wolf 3\nL24@P2: Thinking Like a Wolf, a Sheep, or a Firefly:\nL25@P2: Learning Biology Through Constructing and Testing\nL26@P2: Computational Theories--an Embodied Modeling Approach\nL27@P2: \"When I observed phenomena in the laboratory that I did not understand, I would also ask\nL28@P2: questions as if interrogating myself: \"Why would I do that if I were a virus or a cancer cell, or the\nL29@P2: immune system\" Before long, this internal dialogue became second nature to me; I found that my\nL30@P2: mind worked this way all the time.\" (Salk, 1983, p. 7)\nL31@P2: There is a sharp contrast between the picture of the field of biology as studied in school\nL32@P2: settings and the picture that emerges from the practice of current biology research.\nL33@P2: While the two pictures are linked by similar content and the objects of study are recognizably the\nL34@P2: same, the processes involved in the two activities are quite different.\nL35@P2: In school settings, typical instruction emphasizes the memorization of classification\nL36@P2: schemas and established theories. In middle school, classification may take the form of learning the\nL37@P2: names of the bones of the body, the names and shapes of different plant leaves or the phyla in the\nL38@P2: animal kingdom. In high school and early undergraduate studies, the content broadens to include\nL39@P2: unseen phenomena such as parts of the cell or types of protozoa, but the processes of memorizing\nL40@P2: classifications remains essentially the same. Similarly, students study biological explanation by\nL41@P2: absorbing established theories about the process of photosynthesis, the Krebs cycle or the\nL42@P2: succession of evolutionary ancestors. Even in cases where the theories are not yet established, such\nL43@P2: as the extinction of the dinosaurs, the alternative theories are presented as competing stories to be\nL44@P2: memorized. And even when students are exposed to research techniques in laboratory work, the\nL45@P2: emphasis is on following a prescribed procedure rather than reasoning from the evidence gathered\nL46@P2: in the procedure.\nL47@P2: This picture contrasts sharply with the picture that emerges from the recent biology research\nL48@P2-3: literature (e.g., Keeling & Gilligan, 2000; Marion, Renshaw, & Gibson, 2000). In this picture, theThinking Like a Wolf 4\nL49@P3: participants are active theorizers. They devise new evidence and gather methods to test their theories.\nL50@P3: Instead of accepting classifications as given, they are seen as provisional theories that are constantly\nL51@P3: reassessed and reconstructed in light of the dialogue between theory and evidence. Participants also\nL52@P3: reason both forwards, by constructing theories that are consistent with the known evidence, and\nL53@P3: backwards, by deducing consequences of theories and searching for confirming/disconfirming\nL54@P3: evidence. In constructing or assessing an account of a biological phenomenon, they focus on the\nL55@P3: plausibility of the mechanism proposed – can it achieve the task assigned it in a biologically feasible\nL56@P3: manner? This assessment of the mechanism often involves reasoning across a range of levels; thus,\nL57@P3: participants ask: is the mechanism constrained by the structure at the molecular, the cellular, the\nL58@P3: organismic and/or the ecological level?\nL59@P3: The contrast between the processes in which these two communities are engaged leads\nL60@P3: biology students to form a misleading picture of the biological research enterprise. Students form\nL61@P3: beliefs that biology is a discipline in which observation and classification dominate and reasoning\nL62@P3: about theories is rare. Furthermore, they believe that learning biology consists of absorbing the\nL63@P3: theories of experts and that constructing and testing their own theories is out of reach1.\nL64@P3: In this paper, we present an approach that attempts to narrow the gap between school\nL65@P3: biology and research biology. The approach centers on the use of innovative computer modeling\nL66@P3: tools that enable students2 to learn biology through processes of constructing and testing theories.\nL67@P3: In recent years, a number of educational research projects (Feurzeig & Roberts, 1999;\nL68@P3: Gobert, Horwitz, Tinker, Buckley, Wilensky, Levy, et al, 2004; White & Horwitz , 1988; Jackson,\nL69@P3: Stratford, Krajcik, & Soloway, 1996; Jacobson & Kozma, 2000; Jungck & Calley, 1985; Lehrer &\nL70@P3: Schauble, 2000; Ogborn, 1999; Roberts & Barclay, 1988) have employed computer-modeling tools\nL71@P3: in science instruction. The approach taken herein differs from these approaches in its use of agent\u0002based modeling languages (aka object-based parallel) that enable students to model biological\nL72@P3: elements at the level of the individual (e.g., individual wolf/sheep) as opposed to aggregate\nL73@P3: \nL74@P3: 1\nL75@P3-4:  For a similar and much fuller account of the gap between school and research physics, see (Hammer, 1994).Thinking Like a Wolf 5\nL76@P4: (differential-equation based) modeling languages that model at the level of the population\nL77@P4: (wolf/sheep populations). This technical advance in modeling languages enables students to employ\nL78@P4: their knowledge of the behavior of individual organisms (or molecules, cells, genes…) in the\nL79@P4: construction of theories about the behavior of populations of organisms. Furthermore, the ability to\nL80@P4: model individual behavior enables students to employ their personal experience with sensing and\nL81@P4: locomoting in the world as initial elements in their models of other organisms. In this way, the well\nL82@P4: known tendency of children to explain biological behavior through personification (see Carey,\nL83@P4: 1986; Hatano & Inagaki, 1987), instead of being seen as a misconception or a limitation to be\nL84@P4: overcome, becomes a building block towards the construction and refinement of plausible biological\nL85@P4: explanations3.\nL86@P4: In previous work, the authors and other agent-based modeling designers (Repenning, 1994;\nL87@P4: Resnick, 1994; Smith, Cypher, & Spohrer, 1994; Wilensky, 1995, 2001); Wilensky & Resnick,\nL88@P4: 1999) have described the approach in a broad inter-disciplinary context. In this paper, we explore\nL89@P4: the use of this approach, specifically, in biology instruction.\nL90@P4: Mathematical Biology and Computer-Based Modeling – In the Field and in the Classroom\nL91@P4: The gap between school biology and research biology can be partially explained by a lag in\nL92@P4: the transfer of newer biological methods to the school setting. Indeed, at all levels from the\nL93@P4: molecular to the ecological, the science of biology has undergone an important shift over the last\nL94@P4: century. As biologists have increasingly availed themselves of the language of dynamic systems to\nL95@P4: model natural phenomena, biology – once an entirely qualitative discipline – has become more\nL96@P4: quantitative4.\nL97@P4: Mathematical models have added precision to biological theories, have increased their\nL98@P4: predictive power, and have been important sources of explanations and hypotheses. The generation\nL99@P4: and refinement of such models has become a pervasive element of modern biological inquiry. Yet,\nL100@P4: \nL101@P4: 2\nL102@P4:  Elsewhere (Tisue & Wilensky, 2004; Wilensky, 2001) we have argued that the availability and ease of use of\nL103@P4: agent-based modeling environments have also enabled scientists to conduct experimental research that was difficult or\nL104@P4: impossible to do with traditional methods. 3\nL105@P4:  For a detailed discussion on misconceptions reconceived, see (Smith, diSessa, and Roschelle, 1994)\nL106@P4-5: 4 For an illuminating discussion of this transformation of the biological field, see (Allen, 1975)Thinking Like a Wolf 6\nL107@P5: despite this virtual revolution in biology practice, the high school and undergraduate biology\nL108@P5: curriculum have scarcely noticed. For most secondary and post-secondary biology students, the\nL109@P5: study of biology remains primarily an exercise in memorization. Due to the formidable\nL110@P5: mathematical prerequisites that quantitative models of biological change have traditionally imposed,\nL111@P5: students below the advanced undergraduate level are given little or no exposure either to dynamic\nL112@P5: models or to the process of modeling biological change. The computational approach presented\nL113@P5: here enables us to give students this exposure, while sidestepping the traditional mathematical\nL114@P5: roadblocks.\nL115@P5: We begin, in the following section, by describing our “embodied” approach to biological\nL116@P5: modeling and the agent-based modeling languages, StarLogoT (Wilensky, 1997b) and NetLogo\nL117@P5: (Wilensky, 1999b), in which the models are constructed. In section three, we illustrate this approach\nL118@P5: and contrast it with classical modeling techniques by developing both embodied and classical\nL119@P5: models of predator-prey population fluctuations. We follow a high school student, Talia, in her\nL120@P5: efforts to create embodied models of wolf-sheep predation. In section four, we follow another\nL121@P5: student, Paul, as he develops a computational model of synchronously flashing fireflies (these\nL122@P5: species of fireflies are prevalent in the far east, especially Thailand). In contrast to the topic of\nL123@P5: predator-prey population dynamics, the firefly flash synchronization problem does not easily admit\nL124@P5: classical approaches and is, thus, unfamiliar to students. We use this example to frame a discussion\nL125@P5: of the student modeling process and the relationship of this process to modeling within science.\nL126@P5: Finally, in our concluding remarks we respond to criticism of our approach and summarize the\nL127@P5: major points of the paper.\nL128@P5: Research Settings\nL129@P5: The student modelers described below were participants in the “Connected Mathematics”\nL130@P5: (Wilensky, 1993, 1995) and, principally, the “Making sense of Complex Phenomena” (MSCP)\nL131@P5: (Wilensky, 1997a, 2001) projects in which students learn about complex systems through\nL132@P5: construction of agent-based models of these systems. The goals of the MSCP project are to\nL133@P5-6: construct computational toolkits that enable students to model complex systems, to study studentsThinking Like a Wolf 7\nL134@P6: engaged in using these toolkits and to make sense of their behavioral dynamics. Research has\nL135@P6: documented the difficulties people have in making sense of emergent phenomena, global patterns\nL136@P6: that arise from distributed interactions, central to the study of complex systems. We have labeled\nL137@P6: the constellation of difficulties in understanding emergent phenomena and constructing distributed\nL138@P6: explanations of such phenomena the deterministic/centralized mindset (Resnick, 1996, Resnick &\nL139@P6: Wilensky, 1993; Wilensky & Resnick, 1995, 1999). In the MSCP project we have worked with a\nL140@P6: wide variety of students, ranging from middle school students to graduate student researchers as\nL141@P6: well as both pre-service and in-service teachers, on moving beyond this mindset to a richer\nL142@P6: understanding of the dynamics of complex systems. The primary research sites are two urban\nL143@P6: Boston high schools. Students from these schools participated in the project as part of their\nL144@P6: classroom work. Undergraduates and pre-service teachers participated in the context of teacher\nL145@P6: education courses at Tufts University. Some students participated through informal contexts,\nL146@P6: pursuing modeling investigations in after-school settings or at the laboratory, housed at the project\nL147@P6: site, the Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling (then at Tufts University,\nL148@P6: now moved to Northwestern University). In the classroom context, students, typically, were involved\nL149@P6: in an extended classroom modeling project led by the classroom teacher and assisted by project\nL150@P6: researchers. The role of the researchers was to document student work through videotaping and\nL151@P6: field notes and to support students and teachers in the use of project materials and modeling\nL152@P6: languages. Examples of such support included bringing in books and websites that might be useful\nL153@P6: to the modelers and disseminating interesting cases as potential sources of models. Project\nL154@P6: researchers also engaged students in structured activities (including participatory simulations not\nL155@P6: involving the computer (Resnick & Wilensky, 1998)) that would foster reflection on the concept of\nL156@P6: emergence. They also provided support to students and teachers on the syntax of the modeling\nL157@P6: language. The computational models described in this paper were built in an agent-based (aka\nL158@P6: multi-agent or object-based parallel) modeling language called StarLogoT (Wilensky, 1997b).5\nL159@P6: \nL160@P6: 5 StarLogoT is one of several variants of the StarLogo modeling language (Resnick, 1994, 1996; Resnick &\nL161@P6-7: Wilensky, 1993; Wilensky & Resnick, 1999) – an extended version of MacStarLogo. StarLogoT and the modelsThinking Like a Wolf 8\nL162@P7: Recently, the first author has developed another agent-based modeling language, NetLogo\nL163@P7: (Wilensky, 1999b), that is a (more sophisticated) successor to StarLogoT. The models described\nL164@P7: herein have been converted to NetLogo.6 As NetLogo is the more recent and more powerful\nL165@P7: environment, for the remainder of this paper we refer to NetLogo and not to StarLogoT. In the next\nL166@P7: section, we describe the workings of the NetLogo language and its advantages for modeling\nL167@P7: biological phenomena.\nL168@P7: The NetLogo Modeling Language\nL169@P7: NetLogo derives from, and has contributed to, recent work in the field of complex systems.\nL170@P7: This field studies the dynamics of systems that are constituted by many interacting elements. Taken\nL171@P7: as a whole, the behavior of these systems can be extremely complex and difficult to predict, though\nL172@P7: their individual elements may be quite simple. Examples can be found in many fields, from physics\nL173@P7: and chemistry to economics and political science. Biology has been a particularly fertile domain of\nL174@P7: complex systems oriented research (Langton, 1994; Kauffman, 1995). Indeed, though much of the\nL175@P7: early work in complex systems theory originated in physics, (and to this day, the primary echo of\nL176@P7: this research in classrooms is in physics classes), in our view, the paradigmatic area for studying\nL177@P7: complexity lies in the study of complex biological systems. Though the brain, the immune system,\nL178@P7: and the behavior of organisms such as ants or bees are all oft-cited examples, in fact, nearly all of\nL179@P7: biology can be considered from a complex systems perspective. Genetic and cellular processes can\nL180@P7: be viewed as the complex outcomes from molecular interactions; organisms and their organs can be\nL181@P7: viewed as the complex outcomes from cellular and genetic level interactions, and ecological systems\nL182@P7: can be viewed as the complex outcomes of interactions between individual organisms. Of course,\nL183@P7: there is causality in the other direction as well; organism behavior can affect cellular and genetic\nL184@P7: level activity, and ecological circumstances can affect the behavior of individuals. Indeed, one reason\nL185@P7: \nL186@P7: described in this paper can be downloaded from ccl.northwestern.edu/cm/. Updated NetLogo versions of these models\nL187@P7: can be downloaded from ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/models.\nL188@P7: 6\nL189@P7:  NetLogo and all the models described here can be downloaded from the NetLogo site at\nL190@P7-8: ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/.Thinking Like a Wolf 9\nL191@P8: complex systems can be so difficult to study is that aggregate level structures can have feedback\nL192@P8: effects on the behavior of the elements of which they are composed.\nL193@P8: NetLogo is a general-purpose (domain independent) modeling language that facilitates the\nL194@P8: modeling of complex systems. It works by providing the modeler with a framework to represent the\nL195@P8: basic elements – the smallest constituents – of a system, and then provides a way to simulate the\nL196@P8: interactions between these elements. With NetLogo, students write rules for hundreds or thousands\nL197@P8: of these basic elements specifying how they should behave and interact with one another. These\nL198@P8: individual elements are referred to as “turtles.” (NetLogo owes the “turtle” object to the Logo\nL199@P8: computer language7). Turtles are situated on a two dimensional grid on which they can move\nL200@P8: around. Each cell on the grid is called a “patch,” and patches may also execute instructions and\nL201@P8: interact with turtles and other patches. Some typical commands for a turtle are: move in a given\nL202@P8: direction, change color, set a variable according to some value, “hatch” new turtles, or look at the\nL203@P8: properties (variables) of other turtles. Turtles can also generate random values, so that they can, for\nL204@P8: example, execute a sequence of commands with a fixed probability. Patches can execute similar\nL205@P8: commands, though they cannot change location. The wide range of commands executable by turtles\nL206@P8: and patches makes it possible to use them to represent many different systems. For example, turtles\nL207@P8: can be made to represent molecules, cells, or individual organisms, while patches can represent the\nL208@P8: medium (whatever it may be) in which they interact.\nL209@P8: Dynamic modeling tools, such as NetLogo, are used to represent changes in the states of\nL210@P8: systems over time. In NetLogo, time is represented as a discrete sequence of “clock-ticks.” At each\nL211@P8: clock-tick, each turtle and patch is called upon to execute the rules that have been written for it.\nL212@P8: Students need not write separate rules for each turtle (or patch) – the power of NetLogo comes\nL213@P8: from the fact that all turtles can execute the very same set of rules at each clock-tick. If all turtles are\nL214@P8: executing the same rules, will their collective behavior not be repetitive and uninteresting? To see\nL215@P8: why this is not the case, it is important to take note of the fact that even though two turtles might be\nL216@P8-9: following the same rules, their behavior could be markedly different. This possibility exists becauseThinking Like a Wolf 10\nL217@P9: the two turtles may have quite different internal properties and may be situated in dissimilar\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn13view5 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/118558a0\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.nature.com/articles/118558a0: (500) Redirected to https://idp.nature.com/authorize?response_type=cookie&client_id=grover&redirect_uri=https://www.nature.com/articles/118558a0 which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)"
